WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:07.353
In Africa, 650 million people don't have electricity,

00:00:07.414 --> 00:00:11.609
don't have access to power, don't have access to an industry.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.374
People who would otherwise have been very productive

00:00:14.375 --> 00:00:16.385
if they'd had the means, if they had the capital.

00:00:16.386 --> 00:00:19.424
It's really important that we don't lose sight of these people

00:00:22.358 --> 00:00:26.670
in our bid to grow our economies and become more industrialized.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.756
One of the toughest problems in the world is:

00:00:29.784 --> 00:00:35.174
how do you bring people out of extreme poverty without wrecking the climate in the process?

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.612
Conventional wisdom says it'lll require the burning of enough fossil fuels to cancel out

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:43.077
any progress the developed world makes on emissions.

00:00:43.078 --> 00:00:45.773
But here in rural Kenya, a small experiment is underway

00:00:47.793 --> 00:00:53.159
to see if it's possible to have the economic uplift without the environmental catastrophe.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.306
What we're contributing here is the potential to grow.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:05.409
So essentially, no one is left behind in the race to development, in the race to making our energy more green.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.446
The first time I had power in my village was in the year 1990.

00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:24.656
Before that, there was no power completely.

00:01:26.089 --> 00:01:32.719
The rural setup, the standards of living were extremely, very, very low.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.450
Zachary Ayieko has spent much of his career trying to bring electricity

00:01:37.451 --> 00:01:39.999
to rural Kenyan villages like the one he grew up in.

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:43.291
As an executive at the national power company,

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:49.712
he helped expand Kenya's electricity access from 18 percent of the population in 2010 to 65 percent in 2018,

00:01:51.532 --> 00:01:54.349
the highest in mainland east Africa.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.719
The Kenyan government has done quite a lot in providing universal power.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:06.248
But they have not had enough resources to be able to provide access to every household in the country.

00:02:06.363 --> 00:02:09.793
The Kenyans who have got no power are based purely in the rural areas.

00:02:11.445 --> 00:02:19.275
Once electricity provided, the economic empowerment of people goes very high.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:24.169
With millions of rural Kenyans still disconnected from power,

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:28.249
a small green energy startup is attempting to bridge the gap.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.187
Powerhive is the first privately licensed utility in Kenya.

00:02:38.188 --> 00:02:43.605
We currently operate about 25 village level microgrids.

00:02:45.199 --> 00:02:48.548
We're generating our power from solar. We're storing that energy in batteries.

00:02:48.549 --> 00:02:56.719
When I started in this business 20 years ago, the price per watt of a solar panel was five, six dollars. Now it's 20 cents.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:01.639
It's opened up a huge opportunity in Africa to deliver energy.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.006
A micro or mini grid like the ones built by Power Hive

00:03:06.302 --> 00:03:09.879
is a power system that supplies enough electricity for a small community.

00:03:10.048 --> 00:03:14.830
Schools and homes get electric light and Internet service.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:19.920
Agricultural labor, like maize milling, doesn't have to be done by hand anymore.

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:25.705
Electric pressure cookers eliminate the need to chop down trees or burn charcoal to make meals.

00:03:25.924 --> 00:03:31.009
All of these things raise the standard of living. But there's one big problem.

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:39.080
When we first came into Kenya, we quickly realized that our consumers are too poor to pay for energy.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:42.770
Minigrids have been around for a long, long time.

00:03:42.771 --> 00:03:48.719
The problem is, is that there hasn't been an economic model to support the long term operations of these grids.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.714
It forced us to have to think about how we can support our customers

00:03:52.715 --> 00:03:55.704
in generating enough income that they can sustain themselves,

00:03:56.174 --> 00:03:59.909
and then also create a good energy customer for us as a business.

00:04:25.639 --> 00:04:29.529
In most rural communities in Kenya, subsistence farmers would keep maybe 20 chicken,

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:33.959
and these would be primarily for eggs they might sell or consume themselves, really.

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:39.064
But electricity, along with some startup capital from Powerhive, has changed all that.

00:04:39.284 --> 00:04:44.527
Powered appliances like heat lamps and incubators can transform chicken rearing into a profitable business.

00:04:45.713 --> 00:04:48.751
It's a virtuous cycle for Powerhive: they give their customers

00:04:50.097 --> 00:04:53.524
the money and guidance to start businesses that use their electricity,

00:04:53.525 --> 00:04:58.249
and then a portion of the income from those businesses pays for the electricity.

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:30.230
You also find that community members who do see successful businesses get inspired to do that themselves.

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:33.955
And all of that wouldn't have happened if they hadn't had the exposure,

00:05:33.956 --> 00:05:37.369
or understood how it can be done, or that it can be done.

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:43.439
Recently, Powerhive has invested in a new business:

00:05:43.513 --> 00:05:48.500
leasing electric vehicles to people in the villages and in nearby Kisii Town.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.113
My name is Brian Odoyo. I'm running the ground operations

00:05:53.258 --> 00:05:55.414
for this EV project in this region of Kenya.

00:05:56.723 --> 00:06:05.139
What we're trying to do here is convert old conventional bikes from petrol engines to electric motors.

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:13.001
Beyond just getting from place to place, motorbikes are key to generating income in this region,

00:06:13.002 --> 00:06:17.540
where they're often used as taxi vehicles or for transporting goods to market.

00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:21.471
And while making the switch from gas to electric is obviously cleaner,

00:06:21.472 --> 00:06:24.319
there are some more tangible benefits as well.

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:29.497
Maintaining electric bikes is way more cheaper than maintaining a conventional bike

00:06:30.823 --> 00:06:31.830
in terms of servicing.

00:06:32.038 --> 00:06:35.566
Electric vehicles, they have fewer moving parts. They don't require oil.

00:06:36.105 --> 00:06:41.005
You know, here people are spending around 10 U.S. dollars every two weeks for the maintenance costs.

00:06:41.578 --> 00:06:45.920
So if we can save somebody one or two dollars a day, it's a very, very big deal.

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:49.580
The way we have planned to roll out the program is

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:54.624
we'll be having swapping stations, whereby riders around a certain area

00:06:54.686 --> 00:06:58.067
would be able to change batteries whenever they run out of batteries.

00:06:58.412 --> 00:07:03.056
And the rate at which we'll be offering the battery swaps will be much less

00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:07.100
than what they pay at the pump for fuel for the same conventional bikes.

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:12.640
It's a brand, brand new bike, never been ridden. Let's see how you like it.

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:16.160
You just turn it on. This is the acceleration.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:22.187
We've just introduced our electric motorcycles to a whole group of local riders.

00:07:23.869 --> 00:07:26.515
The market here is really, really been very receptive,

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:31.494
namely because it saves them money and because it performs better than what they're used to using.

00:07:31.648 --> 00:07:34.373
It is so nice! Thank you.

00:07:34.442 --> 00:07:36.987
You're seeing this whole revolution, you could almost call it,

00:07:36.988 --> 00:07:41.569
in terms of this adoption of electric mobility all over the world.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:47.419
So it's just a matter of time before that adoption happens here and everywhere else in the world. I'm convinced of it.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:56.350
It's still early days for minigrid companies like Powerhive, and it remains to be seen how big the industry's impact can be.

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:04.261
A study by BloombergNEF found that electricity from solar hybrid minigrids is still expensive for many rural customers,

00:08:04.868 --> 00:08:07.269
and the sector suffers from a lack of investment.

00:08:07.655 --> 00:08:09.909
Meanwhile, large-scale renewables are booming.

00:08:11.062 --> 00:08:15.717
Bulk solar and wind are the most competitive new sources of energy and most of the world today.

00:08:16.373 --> 00:08:21.604
But with hundreds of millions of people still living beyond the reach of major grids,

00:08:21.659 --> 00:08:26.630
there's a massive opportunity to improve lives without the climate paying the cost.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:31.856
Our business model, if it works, you know, can really change what development means.

00:08:32.204 --> 00:08:36.703
And what we're trying to do is create a model that can be deployed

00:08:36.735 --> 00:08:39.539
anywhere in rural communities across the globe.

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:44.719
We have hundreds of millions of people that are going to be joining the modern economy.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.660
And these customers cannot do so in a carbon unfriendly way.

00:08:50.109 --> 00:08:54.727
And here in Kenya, we're showing that you can sort of create this model,

00:08:54.766 --> 00:08:58.690
which, you know, we hope is actually going to serve Africa for generations.

